  "raw": {
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c01330c8-9bdc-4d7b-b355-90630c559bae&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201299635680_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Pharmacy Administration",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\n\nLocation: Fort Wayne\n\nAssistant/Associate Professor of Pharmaceutical and Graduate Life Sciences (Pharmacy Administration) Make a Difference. Shape Future Pharmacists. Find More Meaning in Your Work. At Manchester University, we're looking for a faculty member who wants to do more than build a career. We're looking for someone who wants to help shape the next generation of pharmacy professionals. Whether you're currently in higher education or considering a transition from industry, this is an opportunity to bring your expertise into a collaborative academic environment where your work has a direct and lasting impact on students, the profession, and the future of healthcare. Unlike large institutions, Manchester offers a close-knit culture where faculty know their students, collaborate across disciplines, and have the opportunity to influence both the curriculum and the student experience. Here, you won't be one of hundreds of faculty members. You'll be a valued colleague and mentor whose work truly matters. Why Consider Manchester? Work with engaged students and help prepare future pharmacists for success. Join a collaborative faculty culture where relationships matter. Enjoy flexible scheduling within a traditional faculty role. Contribute to teaching, scholarship, and service in a supportive environment. Pursue meaningful research and professional interests. Benefit from more than four weeks of paid time off and university holidays each year. Access valuable tuition benefits for eligible family members. Experience the satisfaction of mission-driven work that improves lives through education. What You'll Do As an Assistant or Associate Professor in Pharmaceutical and Graduate Life Sciences, you'll teach and mentor pharmacy students while contributing to scholarship and service within the School of Pharmacy. Teaching responsibilities include pharmacy social and administrative sciences courses, with opportunities to contribute to curriculum development, innovative instruction, and student success initiatives. Candidates with interests in pharmacy administration, health outcomes research, and the teacher-scholar model are especially encouraged to apply. What We're Looking For Qualified candidates will possess credentials supporting appointment at the Assistant or Associate Professor rank, including a Ph.D. or terminal degree in pharmaceutical sciences or a closely related field with a focus on social and administrative sciences. We welcome applications from individuals with academic experience as well as professionals who have built successful careers in industry and are ready for a new chapter focused on education, mentorship, and meaningful impact. Join Us Manchester University is guided by a mission of respect, service, integrity, and lifelong learning. If you're looking for an opportunity to combine your expertise with purpose, we'd love to hear from you.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c01330c8-9bdc-4d7b-b355-90630c559bae&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201299589260_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Maintenance Technician - North Manchester & Fort Wayne Campuses",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\n\nLocation: North Manchester\n\nLooking for a role where you can work independently, solve problems, and make a visible impact every day? We'd love to meet you. Not everyone wants to sit at a desk all day. Not everyone wants the same routine every day. At Manchester University, we're looking for a Maintenance Technician who enjoys solving problems, improving spaces, and taking pride in a job well done. The right person takes initiative, identifies opportunities for improvement, and works independently while collaborating with the team to keep our campuses operating at their best. This is a unique opportunity to work across both our North Manchester and Fort Wayne campuses, spending approximately three days each week in North Manchester and two days in Fort Wayne. You'll help maintain the environments where students learn, faculty teach, and our university community thrives. Responsibilities include building maintenance, repairs, preventive maintenance, facility improvements, and special projects across both campuses.  What You'll Do Maintain and repair university buildings, equipment, and systems Perform plumbing, electrical, carpentry, and general maintenance work Complete preventive maintenance and identify issues before they become costly repairs Assist with construction, remodeling, and improvement projects Support campus events and facility operations Work independently while collaborating with faculty, staff, students, and contractors Help create safe, welcoming, and functional campus environments Travel regularly between our North Manchester and Fort Wayne campuses  You're Probably a Great Fit If You... Enjoy solving problems and figuring things out Take initiative and look for ways to improve things Are dependable, self-motivated, and able to work independently Like variety in your workday and don't mind changing priorities Are comfortable traveling between North Manchester and Fort Wayne Have experience in maintenance, construction, HVAC, facilities, mechanical systems, manufacturing maintenance, or another hands-on field Value teamwork, customer service, and being part of a larger mission We welcome candidates from a variety of maintenance, trades, facilities, construction, manufacturing, and technical backgrounds. If you're excited about this opportunity but don't meet every qualification, we encourage you to apply. Why Manchester University? At Manchester, you'll find more than a job. You'll join a mission-driven community dedicated to helping students discover their purpose and achieve their goals. We offer: Competitive pay Generous paid time off and holidays (over 4 weeks off each year!) Tuition benefits for employees and eligible family members Excellent medical, dental, and vision insurance Retirement benefits Stable, year-round employment A supportive team environment Meaningful work that directly impacts students and the university community Ready to Make an Impact? If you're someone who enjoys hands-on work, takes ownership of your responsibilities, and wants to be part of a university community that makes a difference, we'd love to hear from you. Apply today and help shape the spaces where learning happens.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c01330c8-9bdc-4d7b-b355-90630c559bae&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201293621523_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Member of Pharmacy Practice",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\n\nLocation: Fort Wayne\n\nPosition: Adjunct Faculty in Pharmacy Practice Department: Pharmacy Practice Campus: Fort Wayne, IN, USA (preferred), Remote possible Target Teaching Date:  January 2027 Compensation for this position is $1,500.00 per load hour.  Position Overview: This is a part-time teaching position adjunct faculty position with additional responsibilities related to the academic program for the Spring semester of 2027. It reports through the department chair to the Dean of the College of Pharmacy.  Essential Job Functions Teach in the Integrated Pharmacotherapy Sequence to second- and/or third-year pharmacy students  Content delivery will be both synchronous on campus and asynchronous distance delivery Follow the Accreditation Council for Pharmacy Education (ACPE) Standards. Serve as the course coordinator for the Patient Safety course Ensure all materials are posted 2 weeks in advance of delivery Maintain regular interactions with enrolled students Meeting assigned classes at the scheduled locations and for the scheduled durations Directing and attending non-class activities, as appropriate Assessing student learning Working in a professional, collegial and team-oriented manner with the University community Maintaining compliance with policies and procedures in the Faculty Manual  Maintaining office hours, as appropriate",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c01330c8-9bdc-4d7b-b355-90630c559bae&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201292442493_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty in Patient Safety (Pharmacy)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\n\nLocation: Fort Wayne\n\nPosition: Adjunct Faculty in Patient Safety (Pharmacy) Department: Pharmaceutical and Graduate Life Sciences Campus: Fort Wayne, IN, USA (preferred), Remote possible Target Teaching Date:  January 2027 Compensation for this position is $1,500.00 per load hour.  This is a part-time teaching position adjunct faculty position with additional responsibilities related to the academic program for the Spring semester of 2027. It reports through the department chair to the Dean of the College of Pharmacy. Essential Job Functions: Develop a Patient Safety course (2 credit hours) appropriate for second-year pharmacy students following the Accreditation Council for Pharmacy Education (ACPE) Standards. Dependent on the candidate, the course can be for on campus and asynchronous distance pathway delivery or asynchronous distance delivery only  Compensation will be dependent on course format Serve as the course coordinator for the Patient Safety course Ensure all materials are posted 2 weeks in advance of delivery Maintain regular interactions with enrolled students Report final grades to the registrar If needed, provide remediation to any student that failed and is eligible for remediation Meeting assigned classes at the scheduled locations and for the scheduled durations Directing and attending non-class activities, as appropriate Administering examinations and assessing student learning Working in a professional, collegial and team-oriented manner with the University community Maintaining compliance with policies and procedures in the Faculty Manual  Maintaining office hours, as appropriate  Qualifications:  Ph.D. in Pharmacy Administration or a related discipline, or a Doctor of Pharmacy degree with demonstrated experience in Pharmacy Administration. Experience teaching, developing, or coordinating pharmacy-related coursework. Strong knowledge of patient safety principles and contemporary pharmacy practice. Excellent communication, presentation, and interpersonal skills. Strong organizational and administrative abilities with attention to detail. Ability to assess student learning and provide constructive feedback. Commitment to supporting diverse learners in a collaborative academic environment. Ability to manage multiple priorities, meet deadlines, and respond effectively to student needs. Proficiency with learning technologies and standard office software applications. To apply , please select the \"Apply\" button to begin the application process and submit a cover letter, curriculum vita/resume, evidence of academic course development, and unofficial transcripts of all earned degrees. Manchester University is an equal opportunity employer, and we are committed to respecting the infinite worth of every individual.  We encourage individuals of all backgrounds to apply.",
      "department": ""
    },
